{
  "concept": "Drawing a correct free-body diagram for stacked blocks on an incline requires isolating one body at a time, including only forces acting on that body, and recognizing that contact forces come in Newton's-third-law pairs while friction opposes the relative sliding tendency at each surface.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "tan incline plane sloping down to the left, with a vertical wall at the top right",
    "small salmon block labeled A resting on larger tan block B",
    "cord attached to the blocks running up the slope toward the wall",
    "rounded outline circling block A (panel a isolation) and block B (panel b isolation)",
    "panel (a) free-body diagram: black dot on a dashed line representing the incline surface",
    "panel (a) red vector N_BA pointing perpendicular to and away from the incline",
    "panel (a) red vector T pointing up the slope",
    "panel (a) red vector f_BA pointing down the slope",
    "panel (a) red vector w_A pointing vertically down",
    "panel (a) legend: w_A = weight of block A; T = tension; N_BA = normal force exerted by B on A; f_BA = friction force exerted by B on A",
    "panel (b) free-body diagram: black dot on a dashed line representing the incline surface",
    "panel (b) red vector N_B pointing perpendicular away from the incline",
    "panel (b) red vector f_AB pointing up the slope",
    "panel (b) red vector f_B pointing up the slope",
    "panel (b) red vector N_AB pointing perpendicular down into the incline",
    "panel (b) red vector w_B pointing vertically down",
    "panel (b) legend: w_B = weight of block B; N_AB = normal force exerted by A on B; N_B = normal force exerted by the incline plane on B; f_AB = friction force exerted by A on B; f_B = friction force exerted by the incline plane on B",
    "panel labels (a) and (b)"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "isolateBody",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "A free-body diagram belongs to exactly one object: clicking block A or B isolates it and shows only the forces exerted ON that body, never forces it exerts on others"
    },
    {
      "id": "thirdLawPairs",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Highlight third-law pairs",
      "default": false,
      "teaches": "N_BA and N_AB (and f_BA and f_AB) are equal-and-opposite contact pairs acting on DIFFERENT bodies — one arrow lives on each diagram, never both on the same one"
    },
    {
      "id": "slidingTendency",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Relative sliding tendency",
      "options": ["A tends to slide down", "A tends to slide up"],
      "default": "A tends to slide down",
      "teaches": "Static friction opposes the relative motion tendency at each contact, so reversing the tendency reverses every friction arrow along that surface — friction has no fixed compass direction"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Isolate block A",
      "narration": "Click block A: its outline and free-body diagram brighten while B fades. Only four forces act ON A — its weight, the tension, and the normal and friction forces from B.",
      "state": { "thirdLawPairs": false, "slidingTendency": "A tends to slide down" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Isolate block B",
      "narration": "Click block B: now five forces appear, including the normal and friction forces that A exerts back on B, plus the contact forces from the incline itself.",
      "state": { "thirdLawPairs": false, "slidingTendency": "A tends to slide down" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Compare the contact pairs",
      "narration": "With both diagrams visible, turn on pair highlighting: N_BA on A and N_AB on B point in opposite directions, as do f_BA and f_AB — third-law partners always live on different bodies.",
      "state": { "thirdLawPairs": true, "slidingTendency": "A tends to slide down" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Reverse the sliding tendency",
      "narration": "Switch the tendency to 'A tends to slide up': the friction arrows at the A–B contact flip direction on both diagrams, showing friction responds to the impending relative motion, not to a fixed direction.",
      "state": { "thirdLawPairs": true, "slidingTendency": "A tends to slide up" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Each free-body diagram contains only the forces acting on the isolated body, and every contact force on one diagram has an equal-and-opposite partner on the other body's diagram.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Two stacked panels, (a) above (b), each a three-column layout: free-body diagram on the left, incline sketch in the center, symbol legend on the right. Incline is a tan wedge sloping down to the left with a vertical wall at the upper right; block A is small and salmon, block B larger and tan beneath it; a thin black cord runs up the slope. All force vectors are red arrows from a black dot sitting on a dashed incline line, labeled in italic serif vector notation (arrow over symbol, subscripts like BA). Panel (a): N_BA up-left perpendicular to slope, T up the slope, f_BA down the slope, w_A straight down. Panel (b): N_B perpendicular away from incline, f_AB and f_B both up the slope, N_AB perpendicular down into the incline, w_B straight down. Legends are plain-text symbol definitions matching the image wording. The rounded isolation outline on the sketch and the active diagram should brighten together when a body is clicked, with the other panel dimmed to roughly a third opacity; third-law pair highlighting should color the paired arrows a distinct accent on both diagrams simultaneously."
}

